---

<CaseStudy
  title="La Borda"
  subtitle="The closest single-building match to this brief."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/La%20Borda%20building%20-%20front%20facade%202.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="La Borda hero"
  heroCredit="via Wikimedia Commons"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Borda_building_-_front_facade_2.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  next={{ href: '/kalkbreite/', label: 'Kalkbreite, Zürich' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      Twenty-eight households on Can Batlló land the city leases for 75 years, organized as a
      cooperative in <em>cession of use</em>: nobody owns a unit, nobody can flip one, everyone owns
      the building together. Lacol — a cooperative of architects, several of them residents —
      stacked six timber stories around a full-height central patio in the Barcelona
      <em>corrala</em> tradition. Access is by open galleries around the patio; the glazed roof
      turns it into a winter greenhouse and, opened in summer, a ventilation chimney. The building
      is a climate machine and a social machine with the same part.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: the ground floor. Street-facing commercial space plus the cooperative's own
      shared kitchen-dining room, laundry, and guest rooms — so the building's cheapest square
      meters carry its social life, and private units can stay modest.
    </p>
  </section>

