  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      Public land on a long lease removes the land cost that kills Bay Area co-ops — look for city
      or district parcels first.
    </li>
    <li>
      One patio can be the circulation, the climate system, and the social room. Don't buy three
      systems where one section works.
    </li>
    <li>
      Shared kitchen, laundry, and guest rooms let private units shrink honestly — affordability by
      design, not subsidy alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cession of use is the tenure translation target: limited-equity co-op plus a land trust gets
      closest under California law.
    </li>
    <li>
      Residents in the design team (Lacol's model) is a process, not a luxury — it is how the
      building stayed cheap and specific.
    </li>
  </Fragment>

